cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository, its code, or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "MAKANI: Regional Inflation Dynamics in Saudi Arabia — Common Temporal Variation and the Limits of Spatial Predictive Dependence"
authors:
  - given-names: Maha
    family-names: Rashed
date-released: 2026-09-27
version: "1.0.0"
license: MIT
url: "https://github.com/maharashed-r/makani-regional-inflation"
keywords:
  - spatial econometrics
  - panel data
  - regional economics
  - inflation
  - Driscoll-Kraay
  - Moran's I
  - Saudi Arabia
  - reproducible research
  - statistical power analysis
abstract: >
  A reproducible dynamic-panel econometric analysis of regional consumer
  price inflation across Saudi Arabia's 13 administrative regions
  (January 2013 - February 2026, General Authority for Statistics data).
  Tests whether regional inflation dynamics primarily reflect common
  temporal variation, local persistence, or spatial predictive dependence,
  using a two-way-fixed-effects dynamic panel model with Driscoll-Kraay
  inference, distributed spatial lags, nonlinear and structural-shock
  extensions, stationarity diagnostics, a full robustness battery, and a
  calibrated Monte Carlo power/minimum-detectable-effect analysis. Finds
  that common temporal variation explains a substantial share of regional
  inflation variance while no robust spatial predictive dependence is
  detected in the available 13-region panel; classifies the spatial
  evidence as weak/suggestive but non-robust and notes that limited
  statistical power (N=13) means small and moderate spatial effects
  cannot be fully ruled out.
